132:| REQ-001 | The scenario file `decision-capture-live-conversation.md` defines stages S0 to S11 in the 065 format, and a second scenario file `decision-capture-kill-switch-conversation.md` defines S12, because a script has one `env` and a run has one scratch project. Each stage names the human turn, the pause before it, what the runner waits for, the answer the human gives and the checkpoint observable with its expected value. S0 is a check-only turn. S8 is two turns, `S8-compact` (`action: compact`) and `S8-ask`, and S9 uses `action: restart`. |
133:| REQ-002 | Every run uses a disposable scratch project with its own project root and state directory. After a run, `git -C <repo> status --short --ignored specs .skilled/skills/.state` and a content hash of the real `.skilled/skills/.state/decision-capture/` match the baseline taken before it, and the runner reports no `REPO_CHANGED`. |
134:| REQ-003 | The Claude row of the matrix is filled from a real interactive Claude Code session, and every stage S0 to S12 carries PASS, FAIL or SKIP with an evidence path that exists. The stage marks map onto the runner's turn outcomes: a subject deviation is a FAIL whose check prints `DEVIATION: <what happened>`, after one retry (`retry: 1`), and a stage that does not apply is a SKIP whose check exits 77 and prints its named blocker. `checkpoints.tsv` here is derived from the two runs' runner `checkpoints.tsv` files, joining `S8-compact` and `S8-ask` into one S8 row. |
135:| REQ-004 | The Gate 3 or other process question the subject asks in S1 appears in no queue file and in neither operator anchor of `decision-record.md`, while the S1 pane capture shows it was asked. |
136:| REQ-005 | The S6 and S10 full-auto saves write one rewritten decision row and one verbatim appendix item per in-scope answer, with each source key once in `consumed.jsonl`. The S11 repeat save leaves `decision-record.md` byte-identical. |
137:| REQ-006 | The Stop-hook nudge lines the pane shows per turn, S1 to S11, match the expected sequence 0,1,1,1,0,0,1,1,1,0,0, and the `<session>.nudge` file holds the expected count after each turn. |
